{
 "section": "04",
 "slug": "04-08-stonehenge",
 "heading": "## 04 — #8 STONEHENGE, ENGLAND",
 "text": "Number eight: Stonehenge, built in phases over more than a thousand years. Its stones didn't all travel the same distance. The giant sarsens came from the Marlborough Downs area, largely West Woods, about twenty miles away. The smaller bluestones came from the Preseli Hills in south-west Wales, roughly a hundred and fifty miles away. Archaeologists believe builders used wooden sledges, timber tracks and big teams on ropes. But the exact routes, overland or partly by water, are still debated. But the strangest part is still coming. In twenty twenty-four, a study in Nature traced the Altar Stone, at the monument's centre, to north-east Scotland, roughly seven hundred kilometres away. How it got here is unknown. Stonehenge's builders dragged their stones. On one Pacific island, people say the stones walked.",
 "text_chars": 815,
 "previous_text": "Moving stone around one island is hard. Try moving it across a country, five thousand years ago.",
 "next_text": "Number seven: the Moai of Rapa Nui, a remote Pacific island that is part of Chile. Nearly a thousand statues were carved here, most from one volcanic quarry, Rano Raraku.",
